module Auth.Simple.Credential exposing ( Credential, Validity(..) )

{-| The generic credential abstraction: a secret a user proves they hold (a
password, a one-time code, ...). Concrete credentials (e.g.
[`Auth.Simple.Credential.Password`](Auth.Simple.Credential.Password)) implement
this interface; workflows like [`Auth.Simple.Login`](Auth.Simple.Login) and
[`Auth.Simple.Change`](Auth.Simple.Change) drive it without knowing which
credential they hold.

@docs Credential

@docs Validity

-}

import Crypto
import Sqlite
import Task exposing ( Task )


{-| The outcome of validating a candidate secret. An expected negative —
the secret doesn't match (`Invalid`), or the credential is temporarily locked
after repeated failures (`Locked`) — is a value, not an error; the `error` type
of a [`Credential`](#Credential) is reserved for real faults (database, crypto).
This mirrors `Token.verify` returning a `Maybe`.
-}
type Validity
    = Valid
    | Invalid
    | Locked


{-| The five operations a credential supports: persist a new secret, read it,
validate a candidate against the stored secret, burn the work of a validation
without one, and remove it.

A `Credential` is a pure bundle of functions — each one is handed the
`Sqlite.Database` and `Crypto.SecureContext` per call (by the workflow that drives
it, e.g. [`Auth.Simple.Login`](Auth.Simple.Login)), so it holds no config of its
own. `stored` is the secret type persisted by `insert`; `candidate` is the secret
type checked by `validate` (they differ when, e.g., a hashed password is stored
but a plain-text one is verified); `error` is the failure type the functions
surface.

`burn` exists for timing equalization: it must cost the same work as `validate`
against a dummy stored secret and never fail, so a workflow can run it when no
user or secret exists and then fail uniformly — keeping "unknown identifier"
indistinguishable from "wrong secret" to a clock-watching attacker. A credential
whose validation is cheap (e.g. an OAuth-style assertion) may implement it as an
immediate success.
-}
type alias Credential error candidate stored =
    { insert : -- Insert a new credential secret into the database
        { userId : String
        , secret : stored
        }
        -> Sqlite.Database
        -> Crypto.SecureContext
        -> Task error {}
    , get : -- Get the credential from the database
        { userId : String
        }
        -> Sqlite.Database
        -> Crypto.SecureContext
        -> Task error stored
    , validate : -- Given a candidate secret, validate it against the stored one
        { userId : String
        , secret : candidate
        }
        -> Sqlite.Database
        -> Crypto.SecureContext
        -> Task error Validity
    , burn : -- Do the work of a validation without a stored secret; never fails
        candidate
        -> Sqlite.Database
        -> Crypto.SecureContext
        -> Task error {}
    , remove :
        { userId : String
        }
        -> Sqlite.Database
        -> Crypto.SecureContext
        -> Task error {}
    }
